---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import "../styles/pages.css";
---
<Base
  title="How we work · India Atlas"
  description="The standing rules of India Atlas: sourcing, revisions, derived series, corrections."
  nav="method"
  dateStamp="October 2026"
  crumbLeft="<b>Method</b>"
  crumbRight="Read this before citing us"
  canonical="https://atlas.thisindianlife.today/method/"
>
<div class="page method spage">

  <div class="titleblock">
    <div class="kicker">Method</div>
    <h1>How we work, <i>in the open.</i></h1>
    <p class="lede">Every number on this site can be traced back to a press release. This page explains the rules we hold ourselves to: so you can hold us to them too.</p>
  </div>

  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">01</span><h2>The standing rules</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ol class="rules">
      <li><div><h3>Every observation is traceable.</h3><p>Each chart names its publisher, the exact release, and the release date. If you cannot find our source, that is our error: write to us at <a href="mailto:bhuvan@bebhuvan.com">bhuvan@bebhuvan.com</a> and we will fix it or take the chart down.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><h3>Derived series are labelled as derived.</h3><p>Some useful series are not published by anyone: core inflation is the classic example. Where we compute a series ourselves, it is drawn dashed and disclosed in the guide's sources and method, every time it appears.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><h3>Conflicts are shown, not reconciled.</h3><p>When two official statistics appear to contradict, CPI against WPI, old base against new, we plot both as separate series and explain the difference. We never average away a disagreement.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><h3>Plotted points are the real points.</h3><p>Our charts connect actual observations with straight lines. We do not smooth or seasonally adjust unless the source does, and we say when it does. Where the source never published a value, the CPI housing group for ten months of 2018-19, we interpolate the index level, flag the affected months in the series metadata, and disclose it in the guide's provenance. A peak on our chart is a number that was actually published.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><h3>Vintages are explicit.</h3><p>Statistics that get revised, GDP above all, are quoted with their estimate vintage. When a series is restated, we show both vintages rather than silently rewriting history. Every vintage we build is committed to git, so the diff of any revision is reviewable.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><h3>We say "we do not know".</h3><p>Where something is not measured, an official core inflation rate, the informal sector's true output, the page says so, instead of inventing a figure.</p></div></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">02</span><h2>Sources we rely on</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>Our primary sources are India's official statistical agencies. <b>MoSPI and the NSO</b> supply prices, the national accounts, industrial production and the big sample surveys (<b>PLFS</b> for jobs, <b>HCES</b> for household spending, the <b>ASI</b> and <b>ASUSE</b> for enterprises), served through the eSankhyiki API. The <b>RBI</b> supplies banking, credit, deposits, interest rates, payments, public finance and the external account, from its Database on Indian Economy. The <b>Office of the Economic Adviser</b> supplies wholesale prices; ministries supply the rest: agriculture for crops, education for colleges (AISHE), health for the family health survey, commerce for trade, new and renewable energy for power capacity. For comparisons with the world we use the <b>World Bank</b>, the <b>UN</b>, the <b>ILO</b> and the <b>BIS</b>. Each guide names the release and the date its latest figure came from.</p>
        <p>A scheduled pipeline fetches each source, keeps the raw download as an audit trail, checks it (dates in order, values in plausible ranges, parts that add up to their totals, our computed figures against published ones) and commits the result. The site is built from those committed files, and every number is baked into the page: your browser never fetches anything. The exact vintage is printed in every footer.</p>
        <p>Some figures are computed by us from published ones rather than read off a headline table, and each is labelled where it appears. <b>GDP growth</b>, for example, is computed from constant-price levels and checked against the published growth table, which is rounded. Core inflation, shares, ratios and per-person figures are computed the same way, and drawn dashed when they are series of our own.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, i</span>
          <b>CPI settles in a month. GDP takes three years.</b> Two official series, two very different revision clocks. Each guide states which kind it deals in.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, ii</span>
          <b>Base years matter.</b> CPI now uses 2024=100 and GDP the prices of 2022-23. The older bases are kept as series of their own, never joined to the new ones, and every chart that crosses a change of base marks it.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">03</span><h2>Corrections</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <p>Errors are corrected on the page, noted in the guide's version line, and, if they changed a conclusion, flagged at the top of the affected section for one full revision cycle.</p>
  </section>

  <footer class="provenance">
    <div class="fine">
      <span>India Atlas, Method</span>
      <span>Revised October 2026</span>
      <span>Set in Newsreader and Libre Franklin</span>
    </div>
  </footer>

</div>
</Base>
